"use strict";(self.webpackChunk_N_E=self.webpackChunk_N_E||[]).push([[9617],{9617:function(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"content":"\\n## Unpack the images from super.img\\n- When you build AOSP, host tools are also built.\\n- Since the Super Image is a Sparse image, it must be converted to raw before unpacking.\\n- The file system varies according to ```BOARD_*_FILE_SYSTEM_TYPE```, and in the case of ext4, it can be mounted with ```mount -t ext4```.\\n- If it\'s <mark>erofs</mark>, a separate tool (e.g., erofsfuse) is required for mounting.\\n\\n1. You need to convert the Sparse Image to a raw image.\\n```sh\\n{\\n    \\"title\\": \\"Sparse image to raw image\\"\\n}\\n---\\n$ file super.img\\nsuper.img: Android sparse image, version 1.0, Total of...\\n  \\n$ out/host/linux-x86/bin/simg2img super.img super_raw.img\\n  \\n$ file super_raw.img\\nsuper_raw.img: data\\n```\\n2. You need to extract the Super Image, which consists of multiple partitions.\\n```sh\\n{\\n    \\"title\\": \\"Extracting partition images from super\\"\\n}\\n---\\n$ out/host/linux-x86/bin/lpunpack super_raw.img extracted\\n$ ls extracted\\noom_a.img   product_b.img   system_dlkm_a.img ...\\n```\\n3. The file system of the images will appear as ext4, and you must add the offset option for mounting.\\n```sh\\n{\\n    \\"title\\": \\"Loop mount with offset options\\"\\n}\\n---\\n$ mkdir vendor\\n$ sudo mount -t ext4 -o loop,ro,offset=0 vendor_a.img vendor\\n$ ls vendor\\napex   bin   build.prop   etc ...\\n```"}')}}]);